WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.150 --> 00:00:03.675
A last word on line, and it’s about staying alive.

2
00:00:03.925 --> 00:00:09.850
A line under load stores a huge amount of energy, and stretchy nylon stores the most.

3
00:00:10.100 --> 00:00:14.900
If that line parts, it snaps back like a whip, with enough force to kill.

4
00:00:15.150 --> 00:00:17.125
That’s called snap-back.

5
00:00:17.375 --> 00:00:22.550
So keep yourself, and your passengers, out of the path of any line under strain.

6
00:00:22.800 --> 00:00:25.425
When does that happen on a charter boat?

7
00:00:26.425 --> 00:00:30.550
When you’re towing, and when the boat surges against her dock lines.

8
00:00:30.800 --> 00:00:34.875
Never stand inside a bight, a loop of line lying on deck.

9
00:00:35.125 --> 00:00:38.025
If it comes tight, it takes your leg with it.

10
00:00:38.275 --> 00:00:41.100
Use a line that’s big enough for the load.

11
00:00:41.350 --> 00:00:47.400
And inspect your lines for chafe, stiffness and sun damage, and replace them before they fail.

12
00:00:47.650 --> 00:00:49.450
That’s lesson eight.

13
00:00:49.700 --> 00:00:52.900
Nylon stretches, polypropylene floats.

14
00:00:53.150 --> 00:00:55.125
Splice what’s permanent.

15
00:00:55.375 --> 00:00:57.475
Springs stop her surging.

16
00:00:57.725 --> 00:01:00.150
And stay out of the snap-back zone.

17
00:01:00.400 --> 00:01:02.575
Next time: anchoring.
